an></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_mk872skfx9vx"></a><span lang=3DEN>DESCRIBING ACTIONS </span=
></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When choosing an action you must also =
describe
how you perform it. This is part of creating the game&#8217;s atmosphere and
bringing the story to life. Perhaps you plunge yourself into the mud, swear=
ing
as you fire your rifle at the creature. Or maybe you crouch down, sneaking
toward the enemy, gripping your knife so tight your knuckles turn white. </=
span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_rmax7aj5my7u"></a><span lang=3DEN>MOVEMENT </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You can use a fast action to position =
yourself
anywhere within your zone. If the enemy is hiding behind cover or located f=
ar
away in the zone, you may have to spend a fast action to get close enough to
make a close combat attack. Entering an adjacent zone also requires a fast
action.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_js9bkj9443be"></a><span lang=3DEN>ATTACKING </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Attacking an enemy requires a skill te=
st.
Unarmed attacks are performed with <b>FORCE</b>. When armed with melee weap=
ons
you use <b>CLOSE COMBAT</b>. For ranged attacks you use <b>RANGED COMBAT</b=
>. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You need one success to hit your targe=
t,
dealing damage as indicated on the weapons tables. The damage value shown is
the number of physical Conditions that afflict the enemy as a result of your
attack. When rolling multiple successes you can choose to deal additional
damage. For each success the opponent suffers another Condition.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_hv50o7j5e2im"></a><span lang=3DEN>RANGED WEAPONS </span></h=
2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'font-family:"Arial Unicode MS=
";
mso-fareast-font-family:"Arial Unicode MS";mso-bidi-font-family:"Arial Unic=
ode MS"'>The
list of ranged weapons indicates the minimum and maximum range of each weap=
on,
measured in zones. A zero means that you can only use the weapon against an
enemy in the same zone. A weapon cannot be used beyond its maximum range. I=
t is
possible to use a weapon below its minimum range, but with &#8722;2 dice.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[if gte=
 vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image18.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1037" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'w=
idth:442.5pt;
 height:356.25pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:square'>
 <v:imagedata src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image041.png" o:title=3D""/>
</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img border=3D0 width=3D590 height=3D475
src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image042.gif" v:shapes=3D"image18.png"><![endif]></sp=
an></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_gefm218sccx7"></a><span lang=3DEN>DODGING AND PARRYING </sp=
an></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When attacked, you can use your fast a=
ction to
parry in close combat, or dodge a gunshot or some other ranged attack. Both=
 of
these are reactions you can use at any time during the round, even if it is=
 not
your turn. This means that you can choose to &#8220;save&#8221; your fast
action in case you need to parry later in the round &#8211; but you can also
use it before your turn.</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You dodge or parry by passing a skill =
test.
You use <b>AGILITY </b>to dodge, and <b>FORCE </b>or <b>CLOSE COMBAT</b> to
parry depending on whether or not you are armed. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Each success deducts one success from =
the
enemy&#8217;s attack. If you deduct all the enemy&#8217;s successes, the at=
tack
misses. By rolling more successes than required you may choose to swap
initiative cards with your enemy. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You must declare a dodge or parry befo=
re the
enemy rolls for their attack.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_3zwe0bjfs9xw"></a><span lang=3DEN>GRAPPLING </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When attempting to grapple your oppone=
nt, you
make a <b>FORCE </b>test. The attack can be parried normally. If you succee=
d,
your opponent is restrained and cannot perform any actions other than tryin=
g to
break free or shouting. Preventing your opponent from speaking requires an
extra success. The opponent is restrained until you let them go or they bre=
ak
free. Once per round, they may use a fast action to break free using a <b>F=
ORCE
</b>roll. You can resist the attempt by spending a fast action (also rolling
for <b>FORCE</b>), turning it into an opposed roll.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_8qaw2ncmbpy9"></a><span lang=3DEN>FLEEING AND CHASING </spa=
n></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You can use the <b>AGILITY </b>skill t=
o flee
from combat. A successful skill test means that you leave the fight. If you
fail, you remain in the same zone. You cannot flee if doing so would require
you to move through a zone occupied by an enemy. An opponent standing in the
same zone as you can try to prevent you from fleeing, spending a fast action
and making an <b>AGILITY </b>test. Each success eliminates one success from
your result. If your opponent eliminates all of your successes, you are for=
ced
to stay in the fight.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_e6htgk7x0ikz"></a><span lang=3DEN>AMBUSH AND SNEAK ATTACK <=
/span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>In order to ambush your opponent, roll=
 for <b>STEALTH
</b>against their <b>VIGILANCE</b>. If successful, you automatically act fi=
rst
(gaining the #1 initiative card, if cards are used). Each extra success add=
s +1
to your first action of the round. If the ambush fails, you are detected, a=
nd
your opponent wins the initiative. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>If there are several ambushers, only o=
ne of
you makes a <b>STEALTH </b>test. The result applies to all of you. In some
situations, the Gamemaster may decide that no skill test is required in ord=
er
to surprise your enemy. You then automatically win the initiative.</span></=
p>

<h1><a name=3D"_9goxmujh0wg"></a><span lang=3DEN>DAMAGE </span></h1>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>By pushing skill tests or taking damag=
e you
may suffer Conditions. The type of damage (mental or physical) determines t=
he
type of Condition that afflicts you. In combat, the weapon&#8217;s damage v=
alue
indicates how many Conditions you get.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_vu96v7gb0qpy"></a><span lang=3DEN>BROKEN </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When you have checked off all physical=
 or
mental Conditions and suffer another one of the same type, you become Broke=
n.
This means that you are incapacitated, and have sustained a critical injury
that threatens to kill you or traumatize you so badly that you may never
recover &#8211; immediately roll on one of the critical injury tables on pa=
ges
65&#8211;66. You and the Gamemaster together decide exactly what happens to
your character, depending on what caused you to become Broken.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_xj3fx6e0nlys"></a><span lang=3DEN>BROKEN BY PHYSICAL CONDIT=
IONS </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>If you become Broken due to physical
Conditions, it can mean that you have passed out, are bleeding heavily, have
broken several bones, or that you experience such severe pain that you lose
control of your body. You automatically fail all tests. All you can do is
mumble through the pain and, if the Gamemaster allows it, crawl to safety. =
If
you suffer further damage while Broken you suffer another critical injury.<=
/span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_t4ipcquzocla"></a><span lang=3DEN>BROKEN BY MENTAL CONDITIO=
NS </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Being Broken as a result of acquiring =
mental
Conditions means that you are overwhelmed by the situation you are in &#821=
1;
perhaps you are suffering from delusions or have lost all hope of survival,=
 or
maybe you no longer know who or where you are. In any case, you have lost t=
he
ability to look after yourself and act with purpose. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>If attacked while Broken by mental Con=
ditions,
you can flee, parry, and dodge, making skill tests as usual to see if you
succeed. You cannot attack enemies or perform rituals, but are otherwise ab=
le
to use your body and move around. You are still able speak, although what y=
ou
say may not be coherent.</span></p>

<h1><a name=3D"_lm53rm5lu8uf"></a><span lang=3DEN>CRITICAL INJURIES </span>=
</h1>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When you become Broken you sustain a c=
ritical
injury. You roll a D66, that is two six-sided dice, after deciding which die
represents the tens and which represents the ones. For example, if you roll=
 a
three on the first die and a six on the other, the result is 36. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>The Gamemaster will inform you of the =
injury
you suffer from, the associated affliction, and if it is fatal. A fatal inj=
ury
means that it must be treated by someone else within a certain time limit
&#8211; or else you will die or remain mentally broken forever. Should that
happen, you discard your player character and create a new one. The Society=
 has
gained a new member. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Even if you receive treatment and ceas=
e to be
Broken, the injury will affect you for the rest of the mystery. Afflictions
affect you negatively, such as impaired vision in one eye or a general sens=
e of
dread that refuses to let up. Insights are superhuman abilities that help y=
ou,
for example by giving you visions of the future or infusing you with power =
that
makes it easier for you to pass a certain skill test. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When you return to your headquarters, =
you must
determine whether your Afflictions and Insights become permanent, or whether
they heal or pass during the return trip to Upsala.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_1067fcx5alv2"></a><span lang=3DEN>AFFLICTION </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Afflictions mean that your body has su=
stained
an injury that will not heal, which makes life more difficult for you. An
Affliction can affect you once per session &#8211; the Gamemaster decides w=
hen.
The Gamemaster can activate your Affliction in the following ways: </span><=
/p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Increased Difficult</b>y: Adds a penalty to a skill test.
The Affliction states which skill it affects, and how many dice are deducte=
d.
Some Afflictions affect Fear tests. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Social Stigma</b>: A non-player character reacts negativ=
ely
to meeting you. Your injury might make them afraid or hostile. The Gamemast=
er
describes what happens. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>My Own Worst Enemy</b>: A situation which you would norm=
ally
be able to handle now automatically requires a skill test. Perhaps you acqu=
ire
a limp and must pass an <b>AGILITY </b>test to make your way up a hill. </s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Loss of Control</b>: The Gamemaster assumes control of y=
our
actions in a certain situation. This only lasts for a brief moment &#8211; =
then
you return to normal. Perhaps your traumatic memories make you question wha=
t a
friend is saying, or gaps in your memory cause you to confuse important pie=
ces
of information? </span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_t11u18a8j5ow"></a><span lang=3DEN>INSIGHT </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Insights change you, giving you supern=
atural
powers beyond anything normal for a human. You can use an Insight once per
gaming session. Insights can be used in the following ways: </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Increased Skill</b>: Adds a bonus to a skill test. The
Insight states which skill it affects, and how many dice are added. </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Vision</b>: You have a supernatural experience, a prophe=
tic
vision, or an intuitive understanding of a problem. This provides a clue you
need to solve the mystery. The Gamemaster sets the scene and gives you a cl=
ue.
In some cases the Insight cannot be used this way. The Gamemaster decides. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Open Their Eyes</b>: You can give another human the abil=
ity
to see vaesen for the duration of one scene. When using this Insight, you m=
ust
either touch the other person or say something that creates a strong emotio=
nal
bond between you. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&middot; <b>Control My Own Destiny</b>: You can use your Insight to
control what an NPC is thinking or doing. The effect only lasts a moment and
you cannot make them harm themself or their friends. The Gamemaster may dec=
ide
that an NPC cannot be controlled at the moment.</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[if gte=
 vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image6.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1036" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'wi=
dth:447.75pt;
 height:376.5pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:square'>
