</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img border=3D0 width=3D597 height=3D502
src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image044.gif" v:shapes=3D"image6.png"><![endif]></spa=
n></p>

<h1><a name=3D"_4d6qt938fgh9"></a><span lang=3DEN>SPECIAL EFFECTS </span></=
h1>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Your player character might end up in,=
 or even
cause, an array of dangerous or frightening situations that are not directly
involved with combat, although they can certainly occur during combat as we=
ll.
The rules for the effects of these situations are presented below. </span><=
/p>

<h2><a name=3D"_jwer9hy6nx71"></a><span lang=3DEN>FEAR </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When encountering vaesen, being expose=
d to
magic, or feeling scared or stressed as a result of seeing something horrib=
le
like dead bodies, it takes effort not to lose control and become Terrified.=
 You
must pass a Fear test. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Choose whether to tackle the situation=
 with
Logic or Empathy. The attribute value indicates how many dice you may roll.=
 Add
additional dice equal to the number of player characters present in the sce=
ne
(maximum +3 dice). Player characters who are Broken or already Terrified do=
 not
count. If you become frightened during combat you use the number of player
characters situated in the same zone as you. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>On failure you can push the roll by ac=
quiring
a mental Condition. Some talents make Fear tests easier to pass. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>The Fear value of the creature, magic,=
 or
occurrence indicates how many successes you need. If successful, you manage=
 to
control your emotions and can act normally. Extra successes have no effect.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>On failure you become Terrified. Wheth=
er you
flee, freeze, faint, or attack on your next turn is up to you (see below). =
When
you become Terrified, you suffer a number of mental Conditions equal to the
Fear value. Each success on your Fear test reduces the number of Conditions
that afflict you by 1. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>After your first turn while Terrified,=
 you may
try again to overcome your fear on each turn. Make the Fear test again (no
action). If you fail, you remain Terrified another round, but you don&#8217=
;t
suffer any further mental Conditions. Even if you keep failing the Fear tes=
ts,
you stop being Terrified when the scene ends.</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[if gte=
 vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image15.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1035" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'w=
idth:415.5pt;
 height:659.25pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:square'>
 <v:imagedata src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image045.png" o:title=3D""/>
</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img border=3D0 width=3D554 height=3D879
src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image046.gif" v:shapes=3D"image15.png"><![endif]></sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>After your first turn while Terrified,=
 you may
try again to overcome your fear on each turn. Make the Fear test again (no
action). If you fail, you remain Terrified another round, but you don&#8217=
;t
suffer any further mental Conditions. Even if you keep failing the Fear tes=
ts,
you stop being Terrified when the scene ends. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Lastly, it should be noted that if you=
 become
Terrified in a situation that isn&#8217;t particularly hazardous, where the
duration of the effect or your exact reaction is irrelevant, the Gamemaster=
 can
let you improvise your reaction and how long it lasts &#8211; for example if
you are examining a mutilated corpse in an otherwise secure location.</span=
></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_cjro6m9ncibv"></a><span lang=3DEN>EXPLOSIONS </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Explosions hit everyone in the same zo=
ne.
Dodge can be used to dive behind cover or into an adjacent zone. Cover redu=
ces
the damage of explosions, but armor does not. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>The damage is determined by rolling a =
number
of dice equal to the Blast Power. The number of successes shows how much da=
mage
is dealt to those hit by the explosion. The roll cannot be pushed. </span><=
/p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You use <b>RANGED COMBAT</b> to throw
explosives up to two zones (Range 1&#8211;2). Extra successes increase the
damage. If you fail the skill test, the explosive charge hits a randomly
selected adjacent zone.</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>The table below indicates the Blast Po=
wer of
different explosives and how many adjacent zones they affect. An explosive
charge with one adjacent zone means that the explosion affects the zone whe=
re
the charge detonates plus one additional zone. Exactly which adjacent zones=
 it
affects is determined at random. Mark the adjacent zones with numbers
corresponding to a six-sided die. If there are three adjacent zones, they a=
re
marked with the values 1&#8211;2, 3&#8211;4, and 5&#8211;6. One die decides
which zone is hit. For each zone away from the place of detonation, the dam=
age
decreases by 1.</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[if gte=
 vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image32.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1034" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'w=
idth:268.5pt;
 height:191.25pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:square'>
 <v:imagedata src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image047.png" o:title=3D""/>
</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img border=3D0 width=3D358 height=3D255
src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image048.gif" v:shapes=3D"image32.png"><![endif]></sp=
an></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_nj322akiph8i"></a><span lang=3DEN>FIRE </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Catching fire causes you to take damag=
e and
suffer a Condition. If the fire is not extinguished, you will keep taking
damage at the start of each subsequent round. The amount of damage depends =
on
the intensity of the fire (see the table below), but also on an automatic <=
b>FORCE
</b>test you make to reduce the damage &#8211; each success deducts 1 point=
 of
damage. The only actions you can perform while burning are attempts to
extinguish the fire, flee, or shout.</span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[if gte=
 vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image29.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1033" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'w=
idth:306pt;
 height:256.5pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:square'>
 <v:imagedata src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image049.png" o:title=3D""/>
</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img border=3D0 width=3D408 height=3D342
src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image050.gif" v:shapes=3D"image29.png"><![endif]></sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Extinguishing the fire is a slow actio=
n and
requires a successful AGILITY test. More intense fires are harder to put out
and require a greater number of successes. The successes are saved between
rounds, which means that you can start extinguishing it on one turn and fin=
ish
on your next. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Each round the fire is not extinguishe=
d, the
Gamemaster rolls a die. A success means the fire spreads and increases by o=
ne
step, until most of the body is burning. If you are Broken by physical
Conditions while suffering from fire, you automatically take critical injury
#64.</span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_1z38jf1moz5d"></a><span lang=3DEN>POISON </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Poison is measured in toxicity. A weak=
 poison
has toxicity 3, a strong one has toxicity 6, and an extremely potent one ha=
s 9
or more. When you are subjected to poison, you and the Gamemaster make an
opposed roll. The Gamemaster rolls as many dice as the poison&#8217;s toxic=
ity,
while you use <b>FORCE</b>. If successful you do not take any damage. If it=
 is
a tie you suffer one Condition. If you fail, you suffer a number of Conditi=
ons
equal to the Gamemaster&#8217;s successes. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>In subsequent rounds you keep acquiring
Conditions, though one fewer for each round. If you are Broken by physical
Conditions while suffering from poison, you automatically take critical inj=
ury
#51. The effect of the poison can be canceled by using an antidote or passi=
ng a
<b>MEDICINE </b>test with as many successes as the poison&#8217;s damage. <=
/span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_94738s30k14h"></a><span lang=3DEN>FALLING DAMAGE </span></h=
2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When you fall three meters or more ont=
o a hard
surface, the Gamemaster rolls an attack against you. They roll as many dice=
 as
the number of meters you fall, minus 2. For each success you get a physical
Condition. Armor offers no protection. </span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_1kikg6nbtufr"></a><span lang=3DEN>HUNGRY, TIRED, THIRSTY </=
span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>If you do not eat, drink, or rest, the
Gamemaster will ask you to make a <b>FORCE </b>test, where failing means you
suffer a Condition. The Gamemaster decides whether it is physical or mental=
.</span></p>

<h1><a name=3D"_2zbqfy4l9zfl"></a><span lang=3DEN>HEALING INJURIES </span><=
/h1>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>After violent or frightening encounter=
s, you
and your friends may need to heal your Conditions. This section describes w=
ays
in which you can help each other. </span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_fz4o5iaq9kpn"></a><span lang=3DEN>TREATING CRITICAL INJURIE=
S </span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>When you have sustained a critical inj=
ury that
might either kill you or become chronic, someone must treat you with <b>MED=
ICINE
</b>or <b>INSPIRATION </b>before the time limit expires. If it succeeds, you
cease to be Broken, while retaining all other Conditions. Extra successes c=
an
be used to heal additional Conditions. If the attempt fails, the person
treating you must either find help, gather more medical supplies, or move y=
ou
to a safer location before they can try again. If no one has successfully
treated you before the time runs out, you either die or become permanently
Broken. Then it is time to create a new player character. </span></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_xqgf0q4p3dc7"></a><span lang=3DEN>MEDICAL AND MENTAL CARE <=
/span></h2>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>You can use <b>MEDICINE </b>to heal ot=
her
people&#8217;s physical Conditions, but not your own. You and your patients
must spend a few hours on treatment, in a (reasonably) secure location.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>Likewise, you can use <b>INSPIRATION <=
/b>to
cure others of their mental Conditions. In addition to spending a few hours=
 in
a safe place, your friends must willingly engage in close contact and dialo=
gue
with you. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>If the skill test succeeds, your patie=
nts heal
a total of three Conditions which you distribute between them. They decide
which Conditions are healed. Broken can also be healed this way. Each extra
success can be used to heal another three Conditions. Only one attempt can =
be
made per day. </span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN>A patient cannot have their physical o=
r mental
Conditions treated by more than one person at a time. However, it is possib=
le
for one person to treat the physical Conditions while another treats the me=
ntal
ones. While you are treating the Conditions of others, you yourself may not
receive treatment. That goes for both physical and mental Conditions.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DMsoNormal><span lang=3DEN style=3D'mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[if gte=
 vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image2.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1032" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'wi=
dth:468pt;
 height:231.75pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:square'>
 <v:imagedata src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image051.png" o:title=3D""/>
</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img border=3D0 width=3D624 height=3D309
src=3D"VaesenPH_files/image052.gif" v:shapes=3D"image2.png"><![endif]></spa=
n></p>

<h2><a name=3D"_okt6p3mcoqsk"></a><span lang=3DEN>RESTING </span><!--[if gt=
e vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"image24.png" o:spid=3D"_x0000_s1027" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" style=3D'p=
osition:absolute;
